@rem Coding Exam 2 (C++): statements and solutions taken verbatim from the exam preparation paper.
@rem Tests are written for this app (self-contained, they print yes/no like Coding Exam 1).

@task cpp-swap-ints
@title swap_ints
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Swap the values of two integers using pointers.

Prototype:
void swap_ints(int *a, int *b);

Example:

int x = 5, y = 10;
swap_ints(&x, &y); // Now x = 10, y = 5

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
void swap_ints(int *a, int *b) {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void swap_ints(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}
@tests
int x = 5, y = 10;
swap_ints(&x, &y);
std::cout << ((x == 10 && y == 5) ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Swaps through the pointers (uses a temporary).
- Does not swap the pointers themselves.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-swap-chars
@title swap_chars
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Swap the values of two characters using pointers.

Prototype:
void swap_chars(char *a, char *b);

Example:

char a = 'A', b = 'B';
swap_chars(&a, &b); // Now a = 'B', b = 'A'

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
void swap_chars(char *a, char *b) {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void swap_chars(char *a, char *b) {
    char temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}
@tests
char a = 'A', b = 'B';
swap_chars(&a, &b);
std::cout << ((a == 'B' && b == 'A') ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Swaps through the pointers (uses a temporary).
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-find-max
@title find_max
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Find the maximum value in an integer array using pointers.

Prototype:
int find_max(int *arr, int size);

Example:

int arr[5] = {3, 9, 2, 8, 5};
int max = find_max(arr, 5); // max = 9

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
int find_max(int *arr, int size) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int find_max(int *arr, int size) {
    int max = *arr;
    for (int *p = arr + 1; p < arr + size; p++) {
        if (*p > max) {
            max = *p;
        }
    }
    return max;
}
@tests
int arr[5] = {3, 9, 2, 8, 5};
int neg[3] = {-4, -2, -9};
std::cout << ((find_max(arr, 5) == 9 && find_max(neg, 3) == -2) ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Starts with the first element as the maximum.
- Walks the array with a pointer, no arr[i].
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-reverse-array
@title reverse_array
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Reverse the contents of an integer array in-place using pointers.

Prototype:
void reverse_array(int *arr, int size);

Example:

int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
reverse_array(arr, 5); // arr = {5, 4, 3, 2, 1}

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
void reverse_array(int *arr, int size) {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void reverse_array(int *arr, int size) {
    int *left = arr;
    int *right = arr + size - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        int temp = *left;
        *left = *right;
        *right = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}
@tests
int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
reverse_array(arr, 5);
bool ok = arr[0] == 5 && arr[1] == 4 && arr[2] == 3 && arr[3] == 2 && arr[4] == 1;
std::cout << (ok ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Swaps elements from both ends moving towards the middle.
- Uses two pointers, no arr[i].
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-strlen-ptr
@title strlen_ptr
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Implement a function that calculates the length of a string (null-terminated character array) using pointers.

Prototype:
int strlen_ptr(char *str);

Example:

char str[] = "Hello";
int length = strlen_ptr(str); // length = 5

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
int strlen_ptr(char *str) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int strlen_ptr(char *str) {
    char *p = str;
    while (*p != '\0') {
        p++;
    }
    return p - str;
}
@tests
char str[] = "Hello";
char empty[] = "";
std::cout << ((strlen_ptr(str) == 5 && strlen_ptr(empty) == 0) ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Walks to the terminating '\0'.
- Returns the pointer difference or a counter.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-count-vowels
@title count_vowels
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Count the number of vowels in a string using pointers.

Prototype:
int count_vowels(char *str);

Example:

char str[] = "OpenAI";
int vowels = count_vowels(str); // vowels = 3 ('O', 'e', 'A')

Note: the example in the question has a small mistake. “OpenAI” contains four vowels (‘O’, ‘e’, ‘A’, ‘I’), so this function returns 4. If your exam says 3, ask your instructor, but 4 is the correct count.

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
int count_vowels(char *str) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int count_vowels(char *str) {
    int count = 0;
    while (*str != '\0') {
        char c = *str;
        if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u' ||
            c == 'A' || c == 'E' || c == 'I' || c == 'O' || c == 'U') {
            count++;
        }
        str++;
    }
    return count;
}
@tests
char str[] = "OpenAI";
char none[] = "rhythm";
std::cout << ((count_vowels(str) == 4 && count_vowels(none) == 0) ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Counts both lower-case and upper-case vowels.
- "OpenAI" has four vowels, so the result is 4.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-copy-string
@title copy_string
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Copy the contents of one string to another using pointers (you may assume the destination has enough space).

Prototype:
void copy_string(char *dest, char *src);

Example:

char src[] = "Hello";
char dest[10];
copy_string(dest, src); // dest = "Hello"

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
@stub
void copy_string(char *dest, char *src) {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void copy_string(char *dest, char *src) {
    while (*src != '\0') {
        *dest = *src;
        dest++;
        src++;
    }
    *dest = '\0';
}
@tests
char src[] = "Hello";
char dest[10];
copy_string(dest, src);
std::cout << (std::strcmp(dest, "Hello") == 0 ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Copies every character and writes the terminating '\0'.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-concat-strings
@title concat_strings
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Concatenate two strings using pointers (you may assume the destination has enough space).

Prototype:
void concat_strings(char *dest, char *src);

Example:

char dest[20] = "Hello, ";
char src[] = "World!";
concat_strings(dest, src); // dest = "Hello, World!"

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
@stub
void concat_strings(char *dest, char *src) {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void concat_strings(char *dest, char *src) {
    while (*dest != '\0') {      // move to the end of dest
        dest++;
    }
    while (*src != '\0') {       // append src
        *dest = *src;
        dest++;
        src++;
    }
    *dest = '\0';
}
@tests
char dest[20] = "Hello, ";
char src[] = "World!";
concat_strings(dest, src);
std::cout << (std::strcmp(dest, "Hello, World!") == 0 ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Moves to the end of dest first.
- Appends src and writes the terminating '\0'.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-sum-array
@title sum_array
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Compute the sum of all elements in an integer array using pointers.

Prototype:
int sum_array(int *arr, int size);

Example:

int arr[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int sum = sum_array(arr, 4); // sum = 10

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
int sum_array(int *arr, int size) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int sum_array(int *arr, int size) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int *p = arr; p < arr + size; p++) {
        sum += *p;
    }
    return sum;
}
@tests
int arr[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
std::cout << (sum_array(arr, 4) == 10 ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Adds every element exactly once, using pointer arithmetic.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-compare-strings
@title compare_strings
@points 10
@lesson 02_pointers
@statement
Description: Compare two strings lexicographically using pointers. Return 0 if they are equal, a negative value if the first string is lexicographically smaller, and a positive value if the first string is larger.

Prototype:
int compare_strings(char *str1, char *str2);

Example:

char str1[] = "apple";
char str2[] = "banana";
int cmp = compare_strings(str1, str2); // cmp < 0 since "apple" < "banana"

No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
int compare_strings(char *str1, char *str2) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int compare_strings(char *str1, char *str2) {
    while (*str1 != '\0' && *str1 == *str2) {
        str1++;
        str2++;
    }
    return (unsigned char)*str1 - (unsigned char)*str2;
}
@tests
char a[] = "apple", b[] = "banana", c[] = "apple";
bool ok = compare_strings(a, b) < 0 && compare_strings(b, a) > 0 && compare_strings(a, c) == 0;
std::cout << (ok ? "yes" : "no") << std::endl;
@expect
yes
@checklist
- Stops at the first difference or at the end of a string.
- Returns negative, zero or positive as specified.
- No use of array indexing (arr[i]) in any function—use only pointer arithmetic.

@task cpp-doubly-linked-list
@title DoublyLinkedList and DoublyLinkedNode
@points 40
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@statement
Implement the linkedlist list code from the lecture notes.

#include <iostream>

class Node {
public:
    int data;
    Node *next;
    Node(int value) : data(value), next(nullptr) {}
};

class LinkedList {
private:
    Node *head;
public:
    LinkedList() : head(nullptr) {}

    ~LinkedList() {
        while (head != nullptr) {
            Node *temp = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete temp;
        }
    }

    void insertAtBeginning(int value) {
        Node *node = new Node(value);
        node->next = head;
        head = node;
    }

    void insertAtEnd(int value) {
        Node *node = new Node(value);
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = node;
            return;
        }
        Node *cur = head;
        while (cur->next != nullptr) {
            cur = cur->next;
        }
        cur->next = node;
    }

    void print() {
        for (Node *cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
            std::cout << cur->data << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }
};

Create a new classes called DoublyLinkedList and a DoublyLinkedNode. DoublyLinkedNode has both a prevPointer and nextPointer

Implement and InsertAtBeginning, InsertAtEnd, and InsertAtPosition

Note (from the answer key): positions are 0-based. A position of 0 or less inserts at the beginning, and a position at or beyond size inserts at the end.
@includes
#include <iostream>
@stub
class DoublyLinkedNode {
public:
    // your code here: data, prevPointer, nextPointer and a constructor taking the value
};

class DoublyLinkedList {
public:
    // your code here: head, tail and size, a constructor and a destructor that deletes every node
    void InsertAtBeginning(int value) {
        // your code here
    }
    void InsertAtEnd(int value) {
        // your code here
    }
    void InsertAtPosition(int value, int position) {
        // your code here
    }
    void print() {
        // your code here: prints 1 <-> 2 <-> 3 <-> 4 <-> null
    }
};
@solution
class DoublyLinkedNode {
public:
    int data;
    DoublyLinkedNode *prevPointer;
    DoublyLinkedNode *nextPointer;

    DoublyLinkedNode(int value)
        : data(value), prevPointer(nullptr), nextPointer(nullptr) {}
};

class DoublyLinkedList {
private:
    DoublyLinkedNode *head;
    DoublyLinkedNode *tail;
    int size;

public:
    DoublyLinkedList() : head(nullptr), tail(nullptr), size(0) {}

    ~DoublyLinkedList() {
        while (head != nullptr) {
            DoublyLinkedNode *temp = head;
            head = head->nextPointer;
            delete temp;
        }
    }

    void InsertAtBeginning(int value) {
        DoublyLinkedNode *node = new DoublyLinkedNode(value);
        if (head == nullptr) {            // empty list
            head = tail = node;
        } else {
            node->nextPointer = head;
            head->prevPointer = node;
            head = node;
        }
        size++;
    }

    void InsertAtEnd(int value) {
        DoublyLinkedNode *node = new DoublyLinkedNode(value);
        if (tail == nullptr) {            // empty list
            head = tail = node;
        } else {
            node->prevPointer = tail;
            tail->nextPointer = node;
            tail = node;
        }
        size++;
    }

    void InsertAtPosition(int value, int position) {
        if (position <= 0) {
            InsertAtBeginning(value);
            return;
        }
        if (position >= size) {
            InsertAtEnd(value);
            return;
        }

        // Walk to the node currently at 'position'
        DoublyLinkedNode *cur = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < position; i++) {
            cur = cur->nextPointer;
        }

        // Insert the new node before cur
        DoublyLinkedNode *node = new DoublyLinkedNode(value);
        node->prevPointer = cur->prevPointer;
        node->nextPointer = cur;
        cur->prevPointer->nextPointer = node;
        cur->prevPointer = node;
        size++;
    }

    void print() {
        for (DoublyLinkedNode *cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->nextPointer) {
            std::cout << cur->data << " <-> ";
        }
        std::cout << "null" << std::endl;
    }
};
@tests
DoublyLinkedList list;
list.InsertAtEnd(2);
list.InsertAtBeginning(1);
list.InsertAtEnd(4);
list.InsertAtPosition(3, 2);
list.print();   // 1 <-> 2 <-> 3 <-> 4 <-> null
@expect
1 <-> 2 <-> 3 <-> 4 <-> null
@checklist
- DoublyLinkedNode holds data, prevPointer and nextPointer.
- DoublyLinkedList keeps head, tail and size.
- Inserting into an empty list sets both head and tail.
- InsertAtPosition handles position <= 0 and position >= size before walking the list.
- Every pointer is updated in both directions; the destructor frees every node.

@set cpp-exam2-part1
@title Coding Exam 2, part 1 - pointer functions (original sample main)
@minutes 60
@description
The ten pointer functions of Coding Exam 2 with the sample main() from the paper at the bottom.
@tasks cpp-swap-ints cpp-swap-chars cpp-find-max cpp-reverse-array cpp-strlen-ptr cpp-count-vowels cpp-copy-string cpp-concat-strings cpp-sum-array cpp-compare-strings
@main
int main() {
    // Test swap_ints
    int x = 5, y = 10;
    std::cout << "Before swap: x = " << x << ", y = " << y << std::endl;
    swap_ints(&x, &y);
    std::cout << "After swap: x = " << x << ", y = " << y << std::endl;

    // Test strlen_ptr
    char str[] = "Hello, world!";
    int length = strlen_ptr(str);
    std::cout << "Length of \"" << str << "\": " << length << std::endl;

    // Test reverse_array
    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::cout << "Original array: ";
    for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        std::cout << *(arr + i) << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    reverse_array(arr, 5);
    std::cout << "Reversed array: ";
    for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        std::cout << *(arr + i) << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}

@set cpp-exam2-part2
@title Coding Exam 2, part 2 - doubly linked list (original test)
@minutes 45
@description
Part 2 of Coding Exam 2 with the test main() from the paper.
@tasks cpp-doubly-linked-list
@main
int main() {
    DoublyLinkedList list;
    list.InsertAtEnd(2);
    list.InsertAtBeginning(1);
    list.InsertAtEnd(4);
    list.InsertAtPosition(3, 2);
    list.print();   // 1 <-> 2 <-> 3 <-> 4 <-> null
    return 0;
}
